.Dd November 20, 2026
.Dt NQSIVENC 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nqsivenc ,
.Nm nqsivdec ,
.Nm nqgsivenc ,
.Nm nqgsivdec ,
.Nm nqcha20
.Nd AES-SIV, AES-GCM-SIV and the ChaCha20 stream
.Sh LIBRARY
libnoqtis, \-lnoqtis
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In noqtis.h
.Ft int
.Fo nqsivenc
.Fa "const uint8_t *key" "size_t keylen"
.Fa "const uint8_t *const *ad" "const size_t *adlen" "size_t nad"
.Fa "const uint8_t *in" "size_t inlen"
.Fa "uint8_t *out" "size_t outlen"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo nqsivdec
.Fa "const uint8_t *key" "size_t keylen"
.Fa "const uint8_t *const *ad" "const size_t *adlen" "size_t nad"
.Fa "const uint8_t *in" "size_t inlen"
.Fa "uint8_t *out" "size_t outlen"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo nqgsivenc
.Fa "const uint8_t *key" "size_t keylen"
.Fa "const uint8_t *nonce" "size_t nlen"
.Fa "const uint8_t *ad" "size_t adlen"
.Fa "const uint8_t *in" "size_t inlen"
.Fa "uint8_t *out" "size_t outlen"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo nqgsivdec
.Fa "const uint8_t *key" "size_t keylen"
.Fa "const uint8_t *nonce" "size_t nlen"
.Fa "const uint8_t *ad" "size_t adlen"
.Fa "const uint8_t *in" "size_t inlen"
.Fa "uint8_t *out" "size_t outlen"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo nqcha20
.Fa "const uint8_t *key" "size_t keylen"
.Fa "const uint8_t *nonce" "size_t nlen"
.Fa "uint32_t ctr"
.Fa "const uint8_t *in" "size_t inlen"
.Fa "uint8_t *out"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn nqsivenc
and
.Fn nqsivdec
are AES-SIV of RFC 5297, deterministic authenticated encryption.
.Fa key
is two AES keys: 32, 48 or 64 bytes for AES-128, AES-192 or AES-256.
.Fa ad
is
.Fa nad
associated strings of
.Fa adlen Ns Bq i
bytes each, authenticated and not encrypted, from none
.Po
.Fa nad
0, when both pointers may be
.Dv NULL
.Pc
to
.Dv NQSIVADMAX
(126).
Each string is a separate input, so the same bytes cut elsewhere are
another message; a nonce, where there is one, is by convention the last.
The output is the 16-byte synthetic IV, then
.Fa inlen
bytes of ciphertext:
.Fa outlen
must be
.Fa inlen
+ 16 for
.Fn nqsivenc
and
.Fa inlen
\- 16 for
.Fn nqsivdec .
No nonce is needed.
The same key, strings and plaintext always give the same output, which
shows when two messages are equal, and nothing else.
.Pp
.Fn nqgsivenc
and
.Fn nqgsivdec
are AES-GCM-SIV of RFC 8452, authenticated encryption that resists a
nonce used twice.
.Fa key
is 16 or 32 bytes,
.Fa nonce
12, and
.Fa ad
the associated data
.Po
.Dv NULL
with length 0 for none
.Pc .
The output is
.Fa inlen
bytes of ciphertext, then the 16-byte tag, with
.Fa outlen
as above.
A nonce should still be new for each message; one used twice with the
same key shows only whether the two messages and their associated data
were equal.
.Pp
.Fn nqcha20
is the ChaCha20 stream cipher of RFC 8439 section 2.4 on its own:
.Fa inlen
bytes of
.Fa in
XORed with the key stream of the 32-byte
.Fa key
and the 12-byte
.Fa nonce ,
from block counter
.Fa ctr
(a block is 64 bytes; RFC 8439 starts a message at 1).
The same call decrypts, and
.Fa out
may be
.Fa in .
.Pp
.Sy \&It is not authenticated .
A changed ciphertext decrypts to a changed plaintext with no error, and
a key, nonce and counter used for two messages give away the XOR of the
two.
It is for protocols that add their own authentication; to encrypt, use
.Fn nqsivenc
or
.Fn nqgsivenc .
.Pp
For the four authenticated functions
.Fa out
may not overlap
.Fa in .
All five take time that depends only on the lengths, never on the key or
the data: AES is computed without tables, and tags are compared in
constant time.
Nothing allocates.
.Ss Constructions
In what follows
.Sy ||
is concatenation,
.Sy xor
is bitwise exclusive or,
.Sy E_K
is AES under the key K (FIPS 197) on one 16-byte block,
.Sy LE32(x)
and
.Sy LE64(x)
are x in 4 and 8 bytes least significant first, and
.Sy 0^n
is n zero bits.
.Pp
AES-SIV (RFC 5297 sections 2.4 to 2.6).
The key is K1 || K2, each
.Fa keylen
/ 2 bytes; K1 is the key of CMAC (RFC 4493), K2 that of the counter
mode.
With A_1, ..., A_n the
.Fa nad
associated strings and P the plaintext:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
dbl(x) = (x << 1) xor (msb(x) ? 0^120 || 0x87 : 0^128)
D_0    = CMAC_K1(0^128)
D_i    = dbl(D_(i-1)) xor CMAC_K1(A_i)        i = 1, ..., n
T      = P xorend D_n                         if |P| >= 16
       = dbl(D_n) xor (P || 0x80 || 0...0)    if |P| < 16 (16 bytes)
V      = CMAC_K1(T)
Q      = V with bit 63 and bit 31 cleared
C      = P xor first |P| bytes of
         E_K2(Q) || E_K2(Q + 1) || E_K2(Q + 2) || ...
out    = V || C
.Ed
.Pp
dbl is doubling in GF(2^128) modulo x^128 + x^7 + x^2 + x + 1, the
block read most significant byte first;
.Sy xorend
XORs D_n into the last 16 bytes of P; Q + i is addition modulo 2^128 of
the block read as a big-endian number; the bits cleared in Q are the top
bits of its bytes 8 and 12 (counting from 0).
Decryption computes P from Q and C, then V' from P, and returns P only
when V' equals V, compared in constant time.
.Pp
AES-GCM-SIV (RFC 8452 section 4).
K is the key of
.Fa keylen
bytes, N the 12-byte nonce, A the associated data and P the plaintext.
With m = 4 for a 16-byte key and m = 6 for a 32-byte one:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
B_j = first 8 bytes of E_K(LE32(j) || N)      j = 0, ..., m - 1
H   = B_0 || B_1                              (16 bytes)
K_e = B_2 || ... || B_(m-1)                   (16 or 32 bytes)
S   = POLYVAL(H, pad(A) || pad(P) || LE64(8|A|) || LE64(8|P|))
T   = E_Ke((S xor (N || 0^32)) with bit 127 cleared)
J_0 = T with bit 127 set
J_i = LE32(LE32^-1(J_0[0..3]) + i mod 2^32) || J_0[4..15]
C   = P xor first |P| bytes of E_Ke(J_0) || E_Ke(J_1) || ...
out = C || T
.Ed
.Pp
pad(X) is X followed by zero bytes to a multiple of 16; |X| is a length
in bytes; bit 127 is the top bit of byte 15.
POLYVAL(H, X_1 ... X_s) is S_s, where S_0 = 0 and
S_j = (S_(j-1) xor X_j) * H * x^-128, each 16-byte block read as a
little-endian element of GF(2^128) modulo
x^128 + x^127 + x^126 + x^121 + 1.
Decryption computes P from T and C, then T' from P, and returns P only
when T' equals T, compared in constant time.
.Pp
ChaCha20 (RFC 8439 sections 2.3 and 2.4).
With K the 32-byte key, N the 12-byte nonce and c the counter
.Fa ctr :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
out[i] = in[i] xor ChaCha20_block(K, c + floor(i / 64), N)[i mod 64]
.Ed
.Pp
where ChaCha20_block(K, b, N) is the 64-byte output of the 20-round
block function for block counter b, so that
.Fa inlen
may be at most 64 (2^32 \- c) bytes.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Dv NQOK ,
or
.Bl -tag -width NQEVRFY
.It Bq Er NQEARG
A key or nonce length that is not one of those above,
.Fa outlen
that is not the length it must be, more than
.Dv NQSIVADMAX
strings, an input to decrypt shorter than 16 bytes, or a pointer that is
.Dv NULL
with a nonzero length.
.It Bq Er NQEVRFY
Decryption: the key, the nonce, the associated strings or the input are
not the ones that were encrypted.
.Fa out
is then all zero.
.It Bq Er NQELIM
.Fn nqgsivenc ,
.Fn nqgsivdec :
a plaintext or associated data over 2^36 bytes.
.Fn nqcha20 :
more bytes than the 32-bit block counter has blocks left.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
uint8_t key[64], out[9 + 16], back[9];
const uint8_t *ad[1] = {(const uint8_t *)"header"};
size_t adl[1] = {6};
int rc = nqrand(key, sizeof key);

if (rc == NQOK)
  rc = nqsivenc(key, sizeof key, ad, adl, 1,
                (const uint8_t *)"a message", 9, out, sizeof out);
if (rc == NQOK)
  rc = nqsivdec(key, sizeof key, ad, adl, 1, out, sizeof out,
                back, sizeof back);
/* NQEVRFY when out, the key or the header were changed */
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr noqtis 3 ,
.Xr nqcteq 3 ,
.Xr nqkseal 3 ,
.Xr nqrand 3
.Sh STANDARDS
RFC 5297, RFC 8452 and RFC 8439.
The tests check every test vector of RFC 5297 appendix A, RFC 8452
appendix C and RFC 8439 appendix A.2, a changed byte anywhere in each
output, key, nonce and associated string, and agreement with OpenSSL on
random inputs.
.Pp
