.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
The key of
.Fn nqsivenc
is two keys: 32 bytes give AES-128 for both the CMAC and the counter
mode
.Pq Dq AEAD_AES_SIV_CMAC_256
of RFC 5297, 64 bytes give AES-256.
A key of
.Fn nqsivenc
is not a key of
.Fn nqgsivenc ,
.Fn nqcha20
or any other function: use one key for one algorithm.
.Pp
AES-SIV is deterministic: equal inputs give equal outputs, so a
ciphertext shows that a message was sent before.
Put a nonce or a counter among the associated strings when that matters.
AES-GCM-SIV with a nonce used twice under one key leaks only equality of
the two messages, but RFC 8452 section 9 bounds how many messages one
key may encrypt with random nonces; a new nonce for each message keeps
its bounds.
.Pp
.Fn nqcha20
gives no integrity at all, and a counter and nonce used twice under one
key give the XOR of two plaintexts.
.Pp
None of the functions hides the length of the plaintext.
